{
  "id": "DrugBank_DB04837 - MONDO_grouped_11786_5324_24332",
  "verbalization": "Clofedanol is implicated in the pathogenesis of Allergic Rhinitis through a series of indirect pathways, all of which involve the treatment of Rhinitis. The drug is associated with Vasomotor Rhinitis, a condition characterized by non-allergic nasal congestion, and is linked to both Mebhydrolin and Pentoxyverine, which are also used for Rhinitis and are known to treat Allergic Rhinitis. These pathways suggest that Clofedanol may exert a modulatory effect on nasal inflammation through the treatment of Rhinitis, potentially by targeting the underlying mechanisms of nasal congestion and inflammatory responses. The evidence is supported by a consistent pattern of indication links, indicating that Clofedanol may be repurposed for Allergic Rhinitis through its role in managing Rhinitis. The mechanism of action is not explicitly detailed, but the presence of multiple intermediate entities suggests a possible involvement in the regulation of inflammatory pathways or nasal mucosal function. The integration of these pathways supports the hypothesis that Clofedanol may have a role in the treatment of Allergic Rhinitis, though further mechanistic investigation is warranted to confirm the direct relationship.",
